Asymmetrical firm limb swelling, Peau d'orange skin changes, Positive Stemmer's sign

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PEL-038
Reason for Visit: Acute worsening of limb swelling, severe heavy, aching pain, and new-onset erythema suspected to be cellulitis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, community nurse
Urgency Justification: The community nurse requested urgent after-hours assessment due to a sudden, painful exacerbation of chronic unilateral limb swelling, now accompanied by spreading erythema and severe functional impairment. The patient is unable to bear weight. Urgent assessment was necessary to differentiate an acute deep vein thrombosis from cellulitis, initiate immediate high-dose antibiotic therapy to prevent sepsis, and manage the severe, restrictive pain prior to the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My left leg has ballooned up completely. It feels like a block of concrete, and the skin is burning and tight."
History of Present Illness: The patient has a clear, profound clinical history explicitly involving extensive regional pelvic lymph node dissection and completely high-dose targeted local radiation therapy for gynecological malignancy. Over the last several months, there has been unilateral, severe, progressive swelling strictly localizing explicitly to the left lower limb. This is characterized by non-pitting, distinctly firm, and highly brawny edema strictly present in the later, definitively chronic stages of the disease. The patient reports a subjective, continuous heavy, deeply aching, and profound restrictive tight sensation explicitly within the completely affected limb. Today, the patient developed a new, severe, deep burning pain and spreading redness. The patient has frequent, recurrent episodes of severe localized cellulitis strictly triggered directly by the fundamentally severely compromised local immune function. There is severe, distinctly unremitting physical functional impairment completely restricting explicitly normal range of motion and essential activities of daily living.
Functional Status: Acutely unable to bear weight on the left leg; mobilizing strictly via wheelchair today.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of systemic sepsis originating from the compromised lymphatic limb; high risk of skin breakdown and ulceration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 102 bpm | Temp: 38.2 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% on room air | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient is uncomfortable, resting in a recliner with the left leg elevated, visibly distressed by the pain and weight of the limb.
