Medication Reconciliation: Oral simple analgesia has been entirely ineffective for the profound, deep aching pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Lymphoedema - Status: Exacerbated (Complicated by Acute Cellulitis)
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) - Cannot be definitively ruled out clinically without a Doppler ultrasound, as DVT often co-occurs with severe pelvic malignancy, but the presence of high fever (38.2 C), sharply demarcated hot erythema, and a history of recurrent cellulitis strongly points to an infectious primary driver tonight.

Malignant external venous compression - Likely contributing chronically to the baseline swelling, but does not explain the acute fever and hot erythema.

Acute heart failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to the strictly unilateral, asymmetrical nature of the profound swelling, and clear lung fields.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly painful exacerbation of chronic, advanced secondary lymphoedema, now severely complicated by acute cellulitis. The objective findings of a 38.2C fever, tachycardia, and a hot, spreading erythematous plaque on a fundamentally immune-compromised limb dictate an immediate infectious crisis. Because the lymphatic system is destroyed, local infections can rapidly disseminate into life-threatening systemic sepsis. Delaying medical assessment until regular clinic hours would result in unmanaged, excruciating pain and a high likelihood of septic shock. Immediate after-hours intervention was absolutely critical to diagnose the infection, commence immediate high-dose antibiotic therapy, and prescribe potent analgesia for the restrictive tissue pain.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered stat intramuscular ceftriaxone to rapidly establish systemic antibiotic coverage, followed by a prescription for high-dose oral flucloxacillin. Prescribed a short course of oral corticosteroids to rapidly reduce the severe, acute localized inflammatory pain and tissue tightness.
Positive Behavioral Change: When resting in the chair (trigger), explicitly place your left leg fully extended on the high ottoman so the ankle is strictly elevated above the level of your heart (behavior). This utilizes gravity to force the stagnant fluid back towards your core, directly preventing further painful stretching of the skin and reducing the heavy, aching sensation (positive consequence).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the community lymphedema nurse specialist to review the compression garments once the acute infection subsides.
DME/Safety (Optional): Cease use of tight compression garments for the next 48 hours to avoid extreme pain and shear injury to the infected skin.
Follow-up: GP review in 24 hours to monitor the erythema margin and assess response to antibiotics.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops a sudden, severe, shaking chill (rigor), the redness spreads rapidly past the knee, the pain becomes unmanageable despite the new medications, or if the patient becomes confused or drowsy, the nurse must call 000 immediately for emergency transfer for suspected sepsis.

